> I like this. I also want some way of testing that the config works,
> before I do the switch.

That is patch 6: "just check" in dotfiles/notmuch. On either host it
checks that mbsync logs in and lists the four folders, that a dry run
of the sync works, that the lab-mail profile has its config, hooks,
and database, and that msmtp reaches lab-mail from the profile's
address. It prints ok or FAIL for each check, and it changes nothing
and posts nothing.

The profile also sits beside your current one. After patches 1-6 you
can sync, index, and read the real list in Emacs with NOTMUCH_PROFILE
set by hand, while posting still goes to the jj maildir. The cover
letter gives the commands for both hosts. I ran the lab-claude steps
against the live lab-mail, and all 12 checks passed.

> pat@p7x.dev

The mail root is ~/mail/pat@p7x.dev. lab-claude's is
~/mail/claude@p7x.dev.

> Yes FCC is good.

Emacs saves your posts by Fcc into ~/mail/pat@p7x.dev/sent. A pass
saves its reply the same way, so lab-process-mail still learns the
outcome from the index, not from anything the agent prints.

> That sounds good. I'm not doing any quarantining right now. I think it's
> reasonable to just skip unknown senders. And log them.

lab-process-mail now answers only pat@patmaddox.com and pat@p7x.dev,
or the addresses in LAB_PROCESS_MAIL_SENDERS. It logs each other
inbox message to stderr once per run, with its id and From, and skips
it. I withdraw "[PATCH 0/2] Quarantine mail from unapproved senders"
[2].

> I think the whole thing is one series, right? Though the host commits
> should be separate from one another.

Yes, it is one series of 14 patches. beastie's commits and
lab-claude's are separate, and so are Emacs, lab-process-mail, and
the notmuch package. They are ordered support, check, switch, and
cleanup, so you can apply the first six and test before you switch.

Two things need your call, and the cover letter has the details:

- The keys. I used M i, M n, M l, and M a in the show, search, and
  tree views. The stock "a" now gets undone by the next notmuch new on
  a message that is still in INBOX and not answered.
- The import. The recipe from v13 puts every old message in INBOX, so
  your inbox would fill with old threads. Importing into Archive
  avoids that.

